# Tutorial 28 — Assets and Performance
# Reference: docs/runtime/systems/asset-management.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_28
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A scene can be correct and still be expensive. Good asset organization and measured performance make the same story work on more machines.
@label lesson_menu
> Paths, catalogs and packs -> assets
> Images, audio and memory -> memory
> Measure before optimizing -> practice
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label assets
Lavender: Keep project-relative asset paths stable. Use consistent case, meaningful folders, and license records. A path that works on one filesystem can fail on another.
Lavender: The runtime supports file assets, catalogs, and ZIP asset packs. Pack directory listings and streamed reads let content be discovered without inflating the entire archive into memory.
Lavender: Package every resource your scripts and menus reference. Test from the exported directory, without the source checkout beside it, so missing dependencies cannot hide.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label memory
Lavender: Compressed file size is not decoded image size. A large transparent layer still occupies a large pixel buffer when loaded.
Lavender: Character composites, lighting variants, and plugin drawings multiply that cost. Use sensible source dimensions and bounded caches; keep expensive preparation off the render thread.
Lavender: Pikupiku's preview host limits its working image to 1080 pixels on the longest edge and caches prepared drawings. Exported preview frames use that same working resolution.
Lavender: Audio loading, decoding, device support, and simultaneous channels also cost resources. Avoid repeatedly restarting the same track or loading large media at every line.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label practice
Lavender: Use the performance HUD and profiler to observe frame time, draw calls, and memory during a representative scene. Include expression changes, lighting, effects, and a scene transition.
Lavender: Exercise: record a baseline, change one expensive asset or effect, and measure again. Test a cold start as well as a warmed cache.
Lavender: Missing assets, failed plugin preparation, and malformed packs should produce useful diagnostics and a recoverable result. Test those paths in a disposable copy.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: Does a tiny compressed PNG always mean a tiny runtime memory cost?
> No, decoded dimensions determine pixel storage -> correct
> Yes, only file size matters -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: Compression saves disk space. The renderer still needs decoded pixels.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Exactly. Measure the resource in the form the runtime actually uses.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_28]
[flag tutorial_done_28]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_6]
